    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Why they happen",
      "heading": "Three reasons for an inspection",
      "html": "<p><strong>The law.</strong> The Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998 say landlords must have gas appliances checked every year by a Gas Safe registered engineer. The Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020 say electrical installations must be inspected at least every five years. The Road Traffic Act 1988 means goods vehicles need roadworthiness inspections under operator licence conditions. Miss these and you may face a crime or lose a licence.</p><p><strong>A contract.</strong> A lease or insurance policy asks for it. Fail, and the policy can be voided or the deal ended.</p><p><strong>Good practice.</strong> A tenancy property check, a pre-purchase survey. They matter when a dispute starts.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "UK rules",
      "heading": "Where the duties come from",
      "html": "<p><strong>Health and Safety at Work Act 1974.</strong> A general duty to keep workplaces, plant and equipment safe. PUWER 1998 says keep work equipment fit for use. LOLER 1998 says lifting equipment needs a thorough examination at set times. The Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 need written schemes of examination.</p><p><strong>Gas.</strong> Landlords must have gas appliances and flues checked every year by a Gas Safe registered engineer. Tenants must get the CP12 within 28 days, and new tenants before they move in.</p><p><strong>Electrical.</strong> Landlords in England must have installations inspected by a qualified person at least every five years. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have separate, similar rules.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Sites and transport",
      "heading": "Sites and vehicles",
      "html": "<p><strong>CDM 2015.</strong> Construction sites must be inspected and watched throughout the work. The principal contractor must make sure workplaces are inspected before each working period, after any event that may have affected stability, and at least every seven days. Record the results.</p><p><strong>Road Traffic Act 1988 and operator licensing.</strong> Goods vehicle operators must keep vehicles roadworthy. That means a recorded safety inspection programme, often every six to thirteen weeks, plus daily walkaround checks. Keep records for at least fifteen months. The Traffic Commissioner can revoke or curtail a licence over missing records.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The record is the inspection",
      "heading": "No report, no protection",
      "html": "<p>The report is the legal proof. It records what you found, what failed and what was done or advised. Keep it for as long as the rules say: fifteen months for vehicle inspection records, the tenancy plus two years for gas safety certificates, and so on.</p><p>As far as the law is concerned, the report is the inspection. That is why you make the record on site, not from memory days later.</p><p>See our <a href=\"/pages/uk-field-compliance-reporting-guide.html\">UK field compliance reporting guide</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>What is the difference between an inspection and an audit?</h3><p>An inspection looks at a real thing, such as equipment, a building or a vehicle, against a set standard. An audit looks at a system or process, such as procedures and policies. A fire extinguisher service is an inspection. A check that your fire risk assessment is up to date is an audit.</p><h3>Who can carry out a compliance inspection in the UK?</h3><p>It depends. Gas inspections must be done by a Gas Safe registered engineer. Electrical inspections need a competent, qualified person working to BS 7671. Vehicle inspections need a qualified person under the operator licence. Often the rules set the competence, not one fixed qualification.</p><h3>What happens if a compliance inspection is not done?</h3><p>It depends on the duty. Skipping a gas safety check can mean prosecution and weakens a landlord's defence against a tenant. Failing to maintain electrical installations can bring HSE or local authority action. Unroadworthy vehicles can cost an operator its licence, and bring criminal liability.</p><h3>Is a compliance inspection the same as a risk assessment?</h3><p>No. A risk assessment looks at risks before they happen. An inspection checks whether a real thing meets a standard right now. They link up: a risk assessment may say an installation needs inspecting, and the EICR may feed the next risk assessment.</p>"
    }
